WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:05.599
So let's begin with the server-client model, and then the high-level concepts that Unreal

2
00:00:05.599 --> 00:00:09.980
introduces when it comes to networked games. We're all familiar with the networking concepts

3
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:14.679
of server and client, so with these in mind, let's take a look at how Unreal structures

4
00:00:14.679 --> 00:00:17.309
its networked games.

5
00:00:17.309 --> 00:00:22.050
In networking terms, a game made in Unreal can take either or both of these shapes,

6
00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:26.269
that being a single player or local multiplayer shape, and the other being a fully networked

7
00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:32.159
multiplayer game. In a single player or local multiplayer game, the game is run locally

8
00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:37.219
as a standalone executable. Players will supply the input to a single computer and

9
00:00:37.219 --> 00:00:41.060
control everything directly, and this means that everything in the game including actors,

10
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:44.560
the world and even the user interface for each of those players will exist on that

11
00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:50.530
local machine. Whereas in a networked game, the server will hold the one true and authoritative

12
00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:55.429
state of the game. In this case, a player will connect to the server, which is more

13
00:00:55.429 --> 00:01:00.030
than likely a different machine, as a client. They will then each remotely control a

14
00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:04.950
pawn that the server provides and that they will own. This pawn and the controller that

15
00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:08.750
possesses the pawn will act as a bridge that allows the player to send remote procedure

16
00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:14.170
calls to the server in order for them to perform any given gameplay action. However,

17
00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:18.439
it is key to note that the server will not stream any sort of visuals to the client.

18
00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:22.760
Instead, the server will simply replicate information about the game to each of those

19
00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:27.239
connected clients. So for example, the server will replicate actors so that the client

20
00:01:27.239 --> 00:01:31.719
will know what actors they need to be able to see as well as replicating any data that will

21
00:01:31.719 --> 00:01:36.790
tell the clients how those actors should behave. Each client in turn will take this data and

22
00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:42.609
simulate a very close approximation of what is happening on the server. So now we can see

23
00:01:42.609 --> 00:01:46.450
that in network games, we suddenly have to deal with a lot more nuance in terms of where

24
00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:52.129
data is stored and what has got authority over it. We essentially have two running processes

25
00:01:52.129 --> 00:01:56.290
and a lot of our time is spent dealing with and managing what process has access to what

26
00:01:56.290 --> 00:02:01.280
information. For instance, the game mode only exists server side whilst all VFX is

27
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:07.219
likely to exist client side. When it comes to servers, Unreal provides two types of

28
00:02:07.219 --> 00:02:11.939
server that we can run. The first is a dedicated server and this runs as a separate

29
00:02:11.939 --> 00:02:17.599
process and it's likely on a remote machine. It does not render any visuals and no clients

30
00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:23.210
are playing locally. It also focuses on maintaining the authoritative state of the game and

31
00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:28.840
it will receive information from clients to verify. The alternative is a listen server

32
00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.159
and this is going to run on the same machine as the client that is hosting it. The hosting

33
00:02:33.159 --> 00:02:37.639
player's client will still run the visuals and these are typically acceptable for small-scale

34
00:02:37.639 --> 00:02:43.270
casual multiplayer or cooperative games. So now that we know how Unreal structures

35
00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:46.550
our networked and single-player games as well as what types of server that we can

36
00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:50.909
deploy, we should take a look at what the connection process to Unreal is like. After

37
00:02:50.909 --> 00:02:55.969
all, a server without any clients isn't actually a networked game. Here we can see a high-level

38
00:02:55.969 --> 00:03:01.099
flow of how we get our client into that game. The first step is to have our client

39
00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:05.300
request a connection to the server. At this point, our server can decide whether

40
00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:09.719
or not to accept that connection at all. Once the connection is accepted, the server

41
00:03:09.719 --> 00:03:14.169
will send information to that client for them to proceed. At this point, the server

42
00:03:14.169 --> 00:03:17.289
will tell the client what map to load and then proceed to wait for that client

43
00:03:17.289 --> 00:03:22.379
to load into that map. Once the server receives this callback, the pre-login function

44
00:03:22.379 --> 00:03:26.180
is called in the game mode and this is a critical step as it allows the game mode

45
00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:31.919
to either accept or reject the connection itself. So carrying on from there, if

46
00:03:31.919 --> 00:03:35.120
accepted, the server will then call the login function in the game mode

47
00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:37.960
and this will create the player's controller on the server which will

48
00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:41.789
then be replicated down to that connecting client. It's also really

49
00:03:41.789 --> 00:03:44.629
important to note that the begin play will happen here for that newly

50
00:03:44.629 --> 00:03:49.560
created player controller. Lastly, the server will call the post login

51
00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.240
function in the game mode and so at this point it's safe to call any RPC

52
00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.879
functions on that player controller. It's fully initialised and we now

53
00:03:55.879 --> 00:03:58.039
have access to any networked functionality.
